// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// RULE: qufl_lock_anchor
// Family: metadata-anchor (Hunt 1 of 5)
// Author pattern: ANCHOR + CONTEXT, thresholds kept explainable
// Works against: PolyglotPoisoning_CL (live) — and conceptually
// against data/practice_ledger.jsonl (offline, via grep/python)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ── THE ANCHOR ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
// قفل ("lock") is a trilateral root (q-f-l). It survives EVERY
// Arabic dialect in the ledger: MSA writes it قفل, Egyptian writes
// it قفل, Tunisian writes it قفل. Dialects change the cells around
// the root; the root does not move. One anchor, three languages.
//
// This is the whole trick of Hunt 1: you do not need to read the
// prompt. You need to know that this root exists and what it means.

PolyglotPoisoning_CL
| where PromptPreview contains "قفل"
| project TimeGenerated, UserPrincipal, Language, Variant, RiskScore, PromptPreview


// ── WITH CONTEXT (the museum-label guard) ──────────────────────
// The anchor alone will also catch a curator writing an honest
// museum label about Victorian locksmithing. That row is benign —
// and it is IN the sample pack on purpose (v2_en_museum_label.txt).
// Context kills the false positive without touching the anchor:
// require the row to ALSO look dialectal (V2) or carry framing words.

PolyglotPoisoning_CL
| where PromptPreview contains "قفل"
| where Variant == "V2" or PromptPreview has_any ("manual", "novel", "step")
| project TimeGenerated, UserPrincipal, Language, Variant, RiskScore, PromptPreview


// ── WITH THRESHOLD (tune the wire) ─────────────────────────────
// The wire trips at 5. Anchored rows under the wire are the
// interesting ones — someone is walking the root in UNDER your
// alarm. Watch the 2–5 band, not just the blocks.

PolyglotPoisoning_CL
| where PromptPreview contains "قفل"
| where RiskScore >= 2 and RiskScore < 5
| project TimeGenerated, UserPrincipal, Language, Variant, RiskScore, PromptPreview
| sort by RiskScore desc


// ── EXPECTED BEHAVIOR ─────────────────────────────────────────
// Against data/practice_ledger.jsonl the anchor returns exactly TWO
// rows — ar-MSA and ar-EG — and nothing else. The ar-TN row in the
// practice data is an exam-cheating prompt that never mentions a lock,
// which is the honest lesson: an anchor catches a ROOT, not a dialect.
// Pick the wrong root and a whole dialect walks past you. On the live
// table the same anchor spans all three Arabic codes, because all three
// carry lock-family prompts there. The museum-label row (en-US,
// framing, score 1.0) demonstrates WHY the context clause exists:
// it contains "lock" and "step" in English and is completely benign.
//
// Offline equivalent (no Sentinel needed):
//   python -c "import sys,json; [print(r['Language'], r['RiskScore'], r['PromptPreview'][:60]) for r in map(json.loads, open('data/practice_ledger.jsonl', encoding='utf-8')) if 'قفل' in r['PromptPreview']]"
//
// A rule you can explain in one sentence is a rule you can defend
// in a report. This one is: "flag any row that mentions the lock
// root, then double-check the ones that look shaped."
